@host = https://localhost:7097

### A single webhook arrives at an unpredictable moment: accepted into the buffer (202).
POST {{host}}/webhooks/stripe
Content-Type: application/json

{ "eventId": "evt_001", "type": "payment.succeeded", "payload": { "amount": 1290, "currency": "eur" } }

### An event whose type ends in ".fail" makes the handler throw: watch the log and the failed counter.
POST {{host}}/webhooks/stripe
Content-Type: application/json

{ "eventId": "evt_002", "type": "payment.refunded.fail", "payload": { "amount": 1290 } }

### Missing fields are rejected before touching the buffer (400).
POST {{host}}/webhooks/stripe
Content-Type: application/json

{ "eventId": "", "type": "" }

### Provider replays a backlog: 200 events from 32 parallel senders. The in-flight window (16) and the
### buffer (64) fill within the first second; senders that wait more than 500 ms for a slot get 429.
### Nobody is dropped silently — the sender is told to retry. Expect a substantial share of rejections.
POST {{host}}/webhooks/shopify/burst?count=200&concurrency=32

### Same backlog from a single sequential sender: every event is accepted, because one writer never waits
### more than ~60 ms for a slot. Backpressure still applies — watch `buffered` sit at 64 while it drains.
POST {{host}}/webhooks/shopify/burst?count=200&concurrency=1

### Small burst with one failure every 10 events: failures visible in /status and in the log.
POST {{host}}/webhooks/shopify/burst?count=40&concurrency=4&failEvery=10

### Live counters: buffered drains at ~16 events/s; runs increments on rotation or broker reconnection.
GET {{host}}/webhooks/status
